## ASSEMBLED SECTION



### <img src="https://flagcdn.com/lt.svg" width="32" alt="Lithuania" style={{display: 'inline', verticalAlign: 'middle', position: 'relative', top: '-2px', marginRight: '8px', border: '1px solid #d1d5db', borderRadius: '2px'}} />Lithuania

Lithuania maintains foundational Western alignment while undergoing accelerated strategic hedging through China rapprochement (Taiwan office renaming proposals) and unprecedented NATO collapse contingency planning, combined with adaptation to European-led NATO command structures. Defense cooperation continues through routine Ukraine support while domestic institutions face persistent but manageable coalition and corruption pressures.

**Key developments:**

- **Diplomatic:** PM Ruginienė stated Lithuania 'rushed ahead of the train' with Taiwan relations and proposed renaming the Taiwanese Representative Office to 'Taipei Office'. President Nausėda responded that diplomatic relations restoration would help communication while maintaining One China policy commitments. China responded that doors remain open for dialogue.
- **Diplomatic:** PM Ruginienė told international media Lithuania needs contingency plans if NATO collapses, emphasizing Europe must prepare for scenarios without US military support. She called for a 'Plan B' and faster EU decision-making in case NATO is politically constrained.
- **Institutional:** United States announced transfer of NATO Naples and Norfolk regional command positions to Italian and British officers respectively, as part of broader Alliance leadership restructuring with European allies assuming greater responsibility for regional defense.
- **Security:** Defense Minister Kaunas announced Lithuania has earmarked €223 million in additional military assistance for Ukraine in 2026, focusing on air defense, drones and long-range artillery ammunition. Lithuania's total military support has exceeded $1 billion.
- **Economic:** PM Ruginienė promised government support for dairy farmers facing rising input costs and price pressures.

<Accordion title="Other Stories">
- **NATO wargame simulates rapid Russian conquest of Lithuania** — A German military simulation showed Russia could potentially overrun parts of Lithuania with 15,000 troops, exploiting weak US leadership and European hesitation. Lithuanian military officials dismissed the exercise as 'nonsense' and criticized its assumptions. *([standard.co.uk](https://politico.com/news/2026/02/13/russia-nato-wargame-germany-simulation-00778818))*
- **TS-LKD appoints Skaistė to lead municipal election campaign** — Opposition party TS-LKD appointed former finance minister Gintarė Skaistė to head their municipal election campaign headquarters. She expressed optimism about the party's prospects, citing gradually improving poll numbers. *([lrt.lt](https://www.lrt.lt/naujienos/lietuvoje/2/2839078/ts-lkd-savivaldos-rinkimu-stabui-vadovaus-gintare-skaiste-ji-prognozuoja-gera-rezultata))*
- **President Nausėda's approval ratings continue six-month decline** — New polling shows President Gitanas Nausėda's approval ratings have been declining for six consecutive months, with the steepest drop among all politicians surveyed, falling 25 percentage points over the past year. *([lrt.lt](https://lrt.lt/naujienos/lietuvoje/2/2833032/apklausa-palankiai-vertinanciu-nauseda-gyventoju-skaicius-mazeja-puse-metu-is-eiles))*
</Accordion>


